---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageSources from '../components/PageSources.astro';
import SourceNote from '../components/SourceNote.astro';

// Orientation page on driving with a foreign licence. Facts verified
// 2026-08-04 against the fetched official sources below. The MIT reciprocity
// list changes often — the page names the stable rules and links the list.
const sources = [
  { title: 'Normattiva — CdS (D.lgs 285/1992), art. 135 (patenti extra-UE e sanzioni)', url: 'https://www.normattiva.it/uri-res/N2Ls?urn:nir:stato:decreto.legislativo:1992-04-30;285~art135!vig=' },
  { title: 'Normattiva — CdS, art. 126, c. 11 (sanzione 158–638 € richiamata da artt. 135 e 136-bis)', url: 'https://www.normattiva.it/uri-res/N2Ls?urn:nir:stato:decreto.legislativo:1992-04-30;285~art126!vig=' },
  { title: 'Normattiva — CdS, art. 195, c. 3 (aggiornamento biennale ISTAT delle sanzioni)', url: 'https://www.normattiva.it/uri-res/N2Ls?urn:nir:stato:decreto.legislativo:1992-04-30;285~art195!vig=' },
  { title: 'Normattiva — CdS, art. 136 (conversione delle patenti estere)', url: 'https://www.normattiva.it/uri-res/N2Ls?urn:nir:stato:decreto.legislativo:1992-04-30;285~art136!vig=' },
  { title: 'Normattiva — CdS, art. 136-bis (patenti UE/SEE equiparate)', url: 'https://www.normattiva.it/uri-res/N2Ls?urn:nir:stato:decreto.legislativo:1992-04-30;285~art136bis!vig=' },
  { title: 'Normattiva — CdS, art. 118-bis (residenza normale: 185 giorni)', url: 'https://www.normattiva.it/uri-res/N2Ls?urn:nir:stato:decreto.legislativo:1992-04-30;285~art118bis!vig=' },
  { title: 'Normattiva — CdS, art. 122 (foglio rosa) e art. 126, c. 11 (sanzione 158–638 €)', url: 'https://www.normattiva.it/uri-res/N2Ls?urn:nir:stato:decreto.legislativo:1992-04-30;285~art122!vig=' },
  { title: 'MIT — Conversione patente estera (elenco Stati, scadenze, costi)', url: 'https://www.mit.gov.it/conversione-patente-estera' },
  { title: 'MIT — Conseguimento patente B (esami, foglio rosa, costi)', url: 'https://www.mit.gov.it/conseguimento-patente-b' },
];
const penalties = [
  ['Non-EU licence, no IDP/translation carried', '€408–1,634 fine (art. 135, c. 8)'],
  ['Resident >1 year, valid non-EU licence', '€158–638; licence seized and sent for conversion — or back home if not convertible (art. 135, c. 14, which sets the amount by reference to art. 126, c. 11)'],
  ['Resident >1 year, expired non-EU licence', 'Treated as driving unlicensed: €5,000+ and a 3-month vehicle fermo (art. 135, c. 11 → art. 116)'],
  ['EU licence expired, or no-expiry licence past the 2-year swap', '€158–638 and the document is withdrawn (art. 136-bis, c. 8, again by reference to art. 126, c. 11)'],
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Driving in Italy on a foreign licence"
  description="EU licences are recognised as-is; non-EU ones work for one year from residence registration, then need conversion or the Italian exams."
  alternates={{ en: '/driving', it: '/it/driving' }}
  ogImage="/og/driving.png"
  citations={sources}
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Driving in Italy on a foreign licence</h1>
    <p>
      Two regimes, one clock. An <strong>EU/EEA licence is recognised as-is</strong>
      (art. 136-bis of the Codice della Strada); a <strong>non-EU licence works
      for one year</strong> counted from the day you register residence — not
      from the day you arrive (art. 135). "Normal residence" is where you live
      at least <strong>185 days a year</strong> (art. 118-bis). Facts verified
      <time datetime="2026-08-04">4 August 2026</time>; orientation, not legal
      advice.
    </p>

  </div>

  <SourceNote module="/driving" />

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>EU/EEA licences: nothing to do — until expiry</h2>
    <p>
      Your licence is <em>equiparata</em> to an Italian one: same rules, same
      penalties, no exchange obligation. Two real duties come with residence:
      Italian <strong>validity and medical-check rules apply to you</strong>, so
      when the licence expires you don't renew it back home — you
      <strong>convert it at the Motorizzazione</strong> (no exams); and an old
      licence <strong>without an expiry date</strong> (or with a longer validity
      than the EU standard) must be converted <strong>within 2 years</strong> of
      taking residence. Optionally you can register (<em>riconoscimento</em>) or
      convert earlier — both bring you into the Italian penalty-points system.
    </p>

    <h2>Non-EU licences: the one-year window</h2>
    <p>
      For one year from <a href="/eu-citizens/residency/iscrizione-anagrafica">residence
      registration</a> you may drive on your valid licence <strong>plus an
      international driving permit or an official Italian translation</strong> —
      carry it, or the fine is €408–1,634. After the year, the licence is no
      longer recognised: you either <strong>convert it</strong> (if your country
      has an agreement) or <strong>take the Italian exams</strong>.
    </p>

    <h2>Conversion without exams: who qualifies</h2>
    <p>
      Conversion exists only with <strong>reciprocity-agreement countries</strong>.
      The MIT list, updated July 2026, includes the <strong>UK</strong> (with
      Gibraltar, Guernsey, Isle of Man and Jersey — agreement to 30 March 2028) and
      <strong>Switzerland</strong> (extended to June 2031). It also covers
      <strong>Japan, South Korea, Brazil, Argentina, Morocco, Tunisia, Turkey,
      Ukraine, Serbia, Albania</strong> and a dozen more.
    </p>
    <p>
      For the <strong>USA, Canada and Chile</strong>, conversion is limited to
      <strong>diplomatic and consular staff</strong> — everyone else takes the
      exams. Check the
      <a href="https://www.mit.gov.it/conversione-patente-estera">official MIT
      list</a>: agreements lapse and return.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>The licence must be <strong>valid</strong> and <strong>obtained before</strong> you became resident in Italy.</li>
      <li>Hard application windows since 2023: <strong>4 years</strong> from residence for some countries (Switzerland, Ukraine, Albania, Argentina), <strong>6 years</strong> for most others (UK, Brazil, Morocco…) — miss it and the application is rejected.</li>
      <li>A licence that was itself obtained by converting a non-convertible one cannot be converted.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How conversion works</h2>
    <p>
      At the <strong>Ufficio Motorizzazione Civile</strong>: form
      <strong>TT2112</strong>, PagoPA payment of <strong>€10.20 + €32.00</strong>
      (tariffa N003), your licence with photocopies, ID,
      <a href="/eu-citizens/residency/codice-fiscale">codice fiscale</a>, two
      photos, and a <strong>medical fitness certificate</strong> (always for
      non-EU conversions; for EU ones when the licence is expiring or has no
      expiry). The foreign licence is withdrawn and returned to the issuing
      state — you keep only the Italian one.
    </p>

    <h2>No agreement? The Italian exams</h2>
    <p>
      Same path as a new driver: <strong>theory test in Italian</strong> (the
      Motorizzazione's candidate guidance offers translation only into German or
      French — <strong>no English</strong>; audio support exists for eligible
      foreign candidates), 2 attempts within 6 months; then the
      <strong>foglio rosa</strong> (12 months) with <strong>6 mandatory
      driving-school hours</strong> before the practical test. Budget roughly
      €42 + €16 in fees plus driving-school costs; the medical certificate is
      required here too.
    </p>

    <h2>If you get it wrong</h2>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="w-full min-w-[480px] border-collapse text-sm">
      <tbody>
        {
          penalties.map(([scenario, sanction]) => (
            <tr class="border-b border-line align-top">
              <td class="py-2.5 pr-4 font-medium">{scenario}</td>
              <td class="py-2.5 text-muted">{sanction}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-muted">
      Treat those euro ranges as this-biennium figures. Under <strong>art. 195,
      c. 3</strong> of the Codice della Strada every fine in the code is
      re-indexed <strong>every two years</strong> by the full ISTAT consumer-price
      variation: a decree by 1 December of each biennium sets the new limits, which
      apply from 1 January. Two of these rows also take their amount by reference
      rather than stating it — art. 135, c. 14 and art. 136-bis, c. 8 both point at
      art. 126, c. 11 — so that is the article to re-read when checking a figure.
    </p>
  </div>

  <PageSources sources={sources} intro="Primary, official sources this page is based on — each fetched and checked on 4 August 2026." />
</BaseLayout>
